WEBVTT
Kind: captions

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:06.080 position:63%
Hi. This is Matt Baker. Welcome to Episode&nbsp;
1 of a new 6-part series that I'll be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:11.040 position:63%
doing using our Timeline of World History&nbsp;
poster. I've covered this chart before on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:15.760 position:63%
this channel but this time I want to go&nbsp;
through things in greater detail—hence a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:20.880 position:63%
series of videos. In this initial episode&nbsp;
we'll be looking at the Early Bronze Age,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:26.640 position:63%
which is shown at the very top of the chart. But&nbsp;
first, let me explain how the timeline works.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:31.600 position:63%
The vertical axis represents time, with&nbsp;
time moving forward as you go down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:37.840 position:63%
page. Every white line represents exactly&nbsp;
100 years — and the scale is equidistant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:41.520 position:63%
meaning that the distance between&nbsp;
two lines is always the same.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.640 position:63%
Meanwhile, the horizontal axis represents&nbsp;
different geographical regions with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:51.840 position:63%
chart roughly going from West to East.&nbsp;
So, on the far left, is the Americas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:58.880 position:63%
followed by Sub-Saharan Africa, Europe,&nbsp;
the Middle East, Asia, and then Oceania.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:03.680 position:63%
Finally, each individual colored line&nbsp;
represents a particular civilization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.360 position:63%
culture, or empire — with&nbsp;
the width representing how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:12.800 position:63%
dominant that civilization was and the&nbsp;
length representing how long it lasted.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:18.640 position:63%
Btw, if you want to get a copy of this chart as&nbsp;
a poster, you can do so right now by clicking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:25.840 position:63%
the link in the description or pinned comment. Or&nbsp;
you can visit us anytime over at UsefulCharts.com.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:30.080 position:63%
Take note that this particular chart is&nbsp;
available in several different formats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:34.800 position:63%
You can get it rolled up and shipped in&nbsp;
a tube (in two different sizes) or you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:38.400 position:63%
can get the new foldable version&nbsp;
(in case you don’t have any wall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:54.080 position:63%
space but still want a copy that you&nbsp;
can pull out whenever you need it).

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:04.080 position:63%
Okay, let's now take a look at the Early Bronze&nbsp;
Age, which started around the year 3300 BCE (which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:10.800 position:63%
is the equivalent of approximately 5,300 years&nbsp;
ago). This date is important because it not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:16.000 position:63%
marks the start of the Bronze Age but also the&nbsp;
start of history in general. This is because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:22.800 position:63%
it’s around 3300 BCE that we get the first&nbsp;
examples of &lt;u&gt;true&lt;/u&gt; writing (due to increased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:27.760 position:63%
urbanization and thus the requirement to keep&nbsp;
detailed records). You see, previous to this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:33.680 position:63%
humans had used written symbols but only to&nbsp;
convey basic ideas (such as in the case of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:40.880 position:63%
these pictograms found at Gobekli Tepe or these&nbsp;
glyphs used by the Vinca culture). True writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:47.280 position:63%
is different in that it uses a set of symbols&nbsp;
to encode all of the various sounds used in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:53.520 position:63%
spoken language so that anything that can be said&nbsp;
out loud can also be written down, word for word.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:58.400 position:63%
Now, obviously humans had existed for a very&nbsp;
long time before they started living in cities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:04.560 position:63%
and writing things down. So the year 3300 BCE&nbsp;
isn’t &lt;u&gt;really&lt;/u&gt; the start of history, it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:11.040 position:63%
just the start of &lt;u&gt;recorded&lt;/u&gt; history. But, by&nbsp;
convention, we use the term “&lt;u&gt;prehistory&lt;/u&gt;” to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:16.800 position:63%
describe events that occurred before the invention&nbsp;
of writing and “history” to describe events that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:22.960 position:63%
occurred after. It just so happened that the&nbsp;
invention of true writing coincided with advances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:28.880 position:63%
in metallurgy so that’s why we call the first&nbsp;
historical period “the Bronze Age” (although,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:34.800 position:63%
take note that during the &lt;u&gt;early&lt;/u&gt; Bronze Age,&nbsp;
copper was actually still the dominant metal).

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:40.640 position:63%
Now, it is often said that there were Six&nbsp;
Cradles of Civilization (meaning six places&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:44.960 position:63%
where, independently from one another,&nbsp;
humans started to organize themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:50.400 position:63%
into more complex societies—basically&nbsp;
meaning societies based around cities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:56.320 position:63%
with things like distinct social classes and a&nbsp;
government responsible for public works (Take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:02.480 position:63%
note that the word civilization itself comes from&nbsp;
the Latin word civitas, meaning city). Anyhow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:10.080 position:63%
the six cradles of civilization were: Sumeria&nbsp;
(in Mesopotamia, or modern-day Iraq), Egypt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:16.640 position:63%
the Indus Valley Civilization, Shang China,&nbsp;
the Olmecs (in what is today Mexico),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:22.720 position:63%
and Norte Chico (in what is today Peru).&nbsp;
Out of these six, China and the Olmecs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:28.160 position:63%
actually emerged a bit later so when it&nbsp;
comes to Early Bronze Age civilizations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:33.280 position:63%
there were really only four. And interestingly,&nbsp;
three of them were located fairly close to each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:39.440 position:63%
other so let’s look at them first. You can see all&nbsp;
three represented here, at the top of the chart:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:46.080 position:63%
the Egyptians in green, the Sumerians in&nbsp;
brown, and the Indus Valley peoples in orange.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:51.680 position:63%
You can also see that trade routes developed&nbsp;
between these three areas almost right away.

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:56.960 position:63%
Now, what all three of these civilizations&nbsp;
had in common, besides increased urbanization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:02.480 position:63%
was that all three developed their own unique&nbsp;
writing systems. You can see examples of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:08.800 position:63%
on our Writing Systems of the World chart. The&nbsp;
Sumerians likely invented their system first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:15.760 position:63%
known as cuneiform. It was made by pressing&nbsp;
wedge-shaped marks into clay. Next came Egyptian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:21.840 position:63%
hieroglyphs which were more picture-like and then&nbsp;
finally, the Indus Valley script (which to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:28.160 position:63%
day, remains undeciphered, despite frequent claims&nbsp;
to the contrary). This means that we have far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:34.400 position:63%
more information about Ancient Sumer and Ancient&nbsp;
Egypt than we do about the Ancient Indus Valley.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:38.400 position:63%
So, moving back to the timeline, there are&nbsp;
a few other things I want to point out about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:45.680 position:63%
each civilization, starting with Egypt. First is&nbsp;
the explanation for why Egypt ended up becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:51.200 position:63%
one of the first civilizations. It basically&nbsp;
boils down to a major change that occurred in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:56.240 position:63%
the Earth’s climate. You see, for thousands of&nbsp;
years before the emergence of Ancient Egypt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:02.960 position:63%
the area that we now know as the Sahara Desert&nbsp;
was much wetter and full of grasslands, lakes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:08.560 position:63%
and forests—meaning that it was a far more&nbsp;
habitable place for humans to live. But then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:13.760 position:63%
just before the start of human history, a&nbsp;
slight change in the Earth’s axis caused the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:19.360 position:63%
region to dry up, eventually turning it into&nbsp;
the Sahara Desert. The result was that a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:24.880 position:63%
more people ended up settling along the Nile&nbsp;
River, where the land could still be farmed.

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:30.640 position:63%
Eventually, came a ruler named Narmer (who some&nbsp;
Egyptologists consider to be the same person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:37.440 position:63%
as Menes). He is credited with unifying&nbsp;
all of Egypt for the first time. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:41.120 position:63%
prior to Narmer (during what’s&nbsp;
called the “pre-dynastic” period),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:48.240 position:63%
there had been a king of Lower Egypt and a king&nbsp;
of Upper Egypt. But Narmer became king of both,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:55.760 position:63%
establishing Dynasty One (which was the first of&nbsp;
31 numbered dynasties that ruled Egypt over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.760 --> 00:07:03.600 position:63%
next 2700+ years). During the first few dynasties,&nbsp;
the kings of Egypt (who we tend to call “pharaohs”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:09.680 position:63%
even though that term wasn’t actually used until&nbsp;
much later) started to be seen as being divine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:15.680 position:63%
And by the time of the fourth dynasty (which marks&nbsp;
the beginning of the Egyptian Old Kingdom), the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:22.080 position:63%
pharaoh sat at the top of a huge bureaucracy that&nbsp;
controlled food production, labour, and religion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:28.080 position:63%
This led to lots of large-scale building&nbsp;
projects—such as the famous pyramids of Giza (near&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:34.480 position:63%
the ancient Egyptian capital of Memphis)—pyramids&nbsp;
which, of course, still stand to this day.

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:40.400 position:63%
The Sumerian civilization, on the other hand, was&nbsp;
a bit different, because it was more a collection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:46.240 position:63%
of city-states rather than a single, unified&nbsp;
kingdom. For a long time, their largest city was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:53.200 position:63%
Uruk, and it was during the period its dominance,&nbsp;
that the Sumerians invented the pottery wheel, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.200 --> 00:08:01.040 position:63%
wagon wheel, the plough, and the sail, and then&nbsp;
finally cuneiform writing. But then, around 2900&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:07.200 position:63%
BCE things really started to take off—around the&nbsp;
same time that there was some sort of major flood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:12.400 position:63%
(which was likely the event that inspired the many&nbsp;
flood myths that became common in this part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:18.160 position:63%
the world, including the famous one found in the&nbsp;
Bible). During the subsequent peak period of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:24.960 position:63%
Sumerian civilization, a major irrigation network&nbsp;
was built and advances were made in arithmetic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:30.800 position:63%
geometry, and algebra (which made their way to&nbsp;
Egypt as well, allowing for the construction of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:37.120 position:63%
the pyramids). Eventually, the city of Ur came&nbsp;
to dominant, which is where this famous box was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:44.160 position:63%
found—overlaid with blue lapis lazuli stone (which&nbsp;
was one of the major trade items at the time).

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:51.600 position:63%
But then, sometime around 2400 BCE a major shift&nbsp;
in the population occurred, due to increased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:57.440 position:63%
migration of Semitic tribes into the area. You&nbsp;
see, unlike most of the people who would go on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:02.560 position:63%
to live in this region throughout history (such&nbsp;
as the Babylonians and Assyrians and then later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:08.720 position:63%
Arabs), the Sumerians did not speak a Semitic&nbsp;
language. Their language is what we call an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:12.880 position:63%
isolate, meaning that it’s not related&nbsp;
to any other language that we know of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:18.800 position:63%
either past or present. The first Semitic&nbsp;
group to dominate the region was the Akkadians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:24.880 position:63%
led by Sargon the Great, who conquered all of&nbsp;
the Sumerian city-states, building the world’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:30.160 position:63%
first empire. You see, the difference between&nbsp;
a kingdom (like the one found in Egypt) and an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:35.280 position:63%
empire (such as the one Sargon built) is that&nbsp;
an empire combines areas with different people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:42.080 position:63%
groups into a single state (in this case, the&nbsp;
Semitic-speaking Akkadians and the Sumerians).

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:46.640 position:63%
Next up, I want to discuss the Indus Valley&nbsp;
civilization (which, like I said earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:52.400 position:63%
remains a bit of a mystery because, while they did&nbsp;
have a writing system, we can’t actually read what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:57.200 position:63%
they wrote). But what we do know is that around&nbsp;
the same time that the Egyptians were building the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.200 --> 00:10:04.000 position:63%
pyramids, and Ur was flourishing in Sumer, some&nbsp;
really large cities (which we call Harappa and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:10.080 position:63%
Mohenjo-dara) were being built by the Indus Valley&nbsp;
peoples. In many ways, these cities were even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:15.520 position:63%
advanced than the ones built by the Egyptians and&nbsp;
Sumerians. You see, rather than focusing on huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:22.000 position:63%
palaces and monuments, the Indus Valley peoples&nbsp;
instead built the world’s first sanitation system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:28.240 position:63%
using standardized bricks that followed streets&nbsp;
laid out in a grid. They also used these highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:33.760 position:63%
standardized stamp seals to label things&nbsp;
(but, again, we don’t know what they say).

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:40.320 position:63%
Now, at this point, I want to talk about the 4.2&nbsp;
kiloyear event, which was a major climate event&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:46.640 position:63%
that contributed to the downfall of both the&nbsp;
Egyptian Old Kingdom as well as the Akkadian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:52.480 position:63%
Empire. The Indus Valley Civilization was&nbsp;
impacted to a lesser degree but overall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:59.280 position:63%
the 4.2 kiloyear event (which happened 4200&nbsp;
years ago) was major enough that the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:05.280 position:63%
it is used as the dividing line between the Early&nbsp;
Bronze Age and the Bronze Age proper (which will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:12.000 position:63%
be the focus of Episode 2). There is still&nbsp;
some debate over what exactly caused the 4.2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:17.840 position:63%
kiloyear event but the consensus seems to be&nbsp;
that it mostly had to do with a shift in ocean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:25.920 position:63%
currents that in turn, led to a major drought&nbsp;
worldwide that lasted for about 100 years.

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:31.840 position:63%
So, it was not only these three civilizations&nbsp;
here that were impacted but also the fourth one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:36.960 position:63%
on the other side of the planet, which was&nbsp;
isolated from the other three. It’s often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:43.040 position:63%
referred to as the Norte Chico civilization&nbsp;
(”Norte Chico” simply meaning “Little North”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:49.440 position:63%
which is a reference to its location just north&nbsp;
of Lima, Peru). It stands out in that while it had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.440 --> 00:12:00.954 position:63%
major cities with large buildings (such as this&nbsp;
one at Caral and this one in the Supe Valley),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.954 --> 00:12:01.520 position:63%
the people living there did &lt;u&gt;not&lt;/u&gt; invent&nbsp;
writing (at least not that we know of). In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:07.680 position:63%
the civilization did not even produce any&nbsp;
pottery, or visual artwork, or items made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:14.160 position:63%
from metal (even though there was lots of copper&nbsp;
nearby). So, like the Indus Valley civilization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:19.840 position:63%
this civilization is a bit of a mystery. We&nbsp;
can only make guesses based on what their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:26.480 position:63%
cities looked like. One feature that stands out&nbsp;
is the lack of walls or other fortifications.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:31.760 position:63%
So perhaps they were peaceful? They also&nbsp;
had these round plazas that were maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:38.160 position:63%
used for spectator events or for special&nbsp;
meetings or ceremonies. We just don’t know.

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:44.000 position:63%
Now, obviously besides the four “civilizations”,&nbsp;
there were lots of other groups of people living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:49.040 position:63%
all over the planet during the Early Bronze&nbsp;
Age. They just weren’t using bronze or living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:54.400 position:63%
in cities but they were still there and&nbsp;
probably doing some very interesting things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.400 --> 00:13:00.560 position:63%
We just don’t know what those things were because&nbsp;
they didn’t leave behind much evidence. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:07.440 position:63%
groups like this are shown on the chart as&nbsp;
dotted lines (such as the Archaic Mesoamericans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:13.840 position:63%
the North American First Peoples, and the&nbsp;
Sub-Saharan Africans). I’ll have more to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:19.920 position:63%
about each one of those groups in Episode&nbsp;
2 because, after the 4.2 kiloyear event,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:26.000 position:63%
we do start to get material culture in those&nbsp;
areas. Notice however, that just for fun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:31.440 position:63%
I do show the Methuselah Tree in this&nbsp;
section. It’s a pine tree located in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:38.960 position:63%
California that is often claimed to be the oldest&nbsp;
still-living non-clonal organism in the world.

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:44.560 position:63%
Moving east, I next want to point out the&nbsp;
Megalith Builders of Malta who were very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:50.320 position:63%
active during the Early Bronze Age. While they&nbsp;
didn’t build cities and thus are not considered a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:57.200 position:63%
“civilization” they did leave behind some massive&nbsp;
stone structures—such as the one shown here, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.200 --> 00:14:04.000 position:63%
was presumably some sort of temple. After Gobekli&nbsp;
Tepe (which is &lt;u&gt;not&lt;/u&gt; shown on this chart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:10.560 position:63%
because it was built way back around 9,500&nbsp;
BCE), the stone temples on Malta are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:16.880 position:63%
oldest free-standing stone structures on earth.&nbsp;
Although there are also some stone structures on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.880 --> 00:14:22.400 position:63%
the island of Orkney, in Scotland that date&nbsp;
to around the same time. Then, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:28.560 position:63%
there’s Stonehenge, in England, but the main part&nbsp;
of that structure (meaning the large stone circle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:34.080 position:63%
seen here) was actually built about 1000 years&nbsp;
later, around the same time as the pyramids.

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:39.760 position:63%
Next up, I want to point out the Indo-Europeans,&nbsp;
who, during the Early Bronze Age, were still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.760 --> 00:14:46.160 position:63%
living in their original homeland. Where exactly&nbsp;
that was is still being debated (although the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.160 --> 00:14:52.480 position:63%
consensus nowadays is that it was likely somewhere&nbsp;
in this red area). Eventually though, near the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:58.480 position:63%
of the Late Bronze Age, they started to spread&nbsp;
out, going in a bunch of different directions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.480 --> 00:15:03.920 position:63%
This purple line represents the groups that ended&nbsp;
up in Western Europe, including the ones who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.920 --> 00:15:10.960 position:63%
became the Celtic peoples, Germanic peoples, and&nbsp;
Italic peoples. In contrast, the blue lines are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.960 --> 00:15:18.080 position:63%
the ones who ended up in Eastern Europe or West&nbsp;
&amp; South Asia, such as the Hellenic branch (which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.080 --> 00:15:25.440 position:63%
became the Greeks), the Anatolian branch (which&nbsp;
became the Hittites), and the Indo-Iranian branch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:30.720 position:63%
(some of whom eventually migrated into North&nbsp;
India where they intermixed with the Indus Valley&nbsp;&nbsp;

